+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`~datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), and `s` (seconds), like `2d`, `90m`, or `1h30m`. Each unit may appear at most once and in that order,
+without spaces. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`.
+
+Negative numbers, decimals, and unknown, repeated, or out-of-order units are rejected. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta`
+passed as a default or from Python is used unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    import datetime
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(),
+                  default=datetime.timedelta(minutes=5))
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=120'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
